#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183905 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34299c34-b199-5100-8fb6-c190eb1db2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square Is Isolated"}] :question/id #uuid "fc7c982c-7e5d-5187-9779-6952f32fe2bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-15019" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression\n$\\left(x^2 + 7x + \\dfrac{49}{4}\\right) + 3$\nas the sum of a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 7x + \\frac{49}{4} &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + 7x + (\\frac{7}{2})^{2} &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x + (\\frac{7}{2})^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (x + \\frac{7}{2})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{2} + 7x + \\frac{49}{4}) + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= (x + \\frac{7}{2})^{2} + 3 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183906 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec6076ff-1538-5bc0-973d-33d908b0b4d2" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/id #uuid "05109436-7f8b-5ef9-a460-b53497a138f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-15020" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 + 7x - 1$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract*\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nwriting the constant term ${\\color{purple}{-1}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - \\dfrac{53}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183907 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1481d4a2-64b4-590a-9dbb-c39d3364bb59" :knowledge-point/title "Expressions Containing a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/id #uuid "2308b4bc-c09f-5f4c-b879-3bb67c92f885" :question/math-academy-id "e-15021" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 - 7x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract*\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x - {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x - {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - \\dfrac{49}{4}\n$$"}]}
